{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Sqrt","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Mass.InvolutionNumbers"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.c8_superexp_sq_lower_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.c8_superexp_sq_lower_bound","kind":"theorem","line":137,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.lean#L137-L167","doc":"**C8 super-exponential lower bound — square form.** The bare `c8_lower_bound`\ncombined with the crude `Ik ≤ kfac` (`involution_le_factorial`) only yields the\n**constant** `w_std ≥ 1/9`. Feeding the *sharper* Gevrey-1/2 involution bound\n`Ik² ≤ 2^K·kfac` instead gives a genuinely **super-exponential** lower bound on the\nsquare:\n\n  `w_std² ≥ kfac / (81 · 2^K)`   ( `= k!/(81·2^k)` at `kfac = k!`, `K = k` ),\n\nand `k!/2^k → ∞` super-exponentially — so `w_std` itself leaves every geometric class.\nSquaring sidesteps `Real.sqrt` entirely: pure algebra over the two proved inputs. This\nis the arithmetic core the C8 audit's \"super-exponential\" claim actually needs (the\n`1/9` constant did not deliver it). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.involution_le_factorial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.involution_le_factorial","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.lean#L112-L133","doc":"`I(n) ≤ n!` — the involution numbers are dominated by the factorial (so the C8\ndenominator `I(k)` keeps `k!/(9·I(k))` at least bounded below, and the super-\nexponential class of `k!/I(k)` is grounded on a concrete sequence). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.c8_lower_bound","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.c8_lower_bound","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.lean#L73-L91","doc":"**C8 super-exponential lower bound.** With `w_std := (Σ f² g)/kfac`, the\nidentity `Σ f g = kfac/3` (input A), and `Σ g ≤ Ik` (input C, `Ik = I(k)`),\nthe Plancherel-weighted branched sum obeys `w_std ≥ kfac/(9·Ik)`. Cauchy–Schwarz is\n`weighted_cs` (proved); only (A)/(C) are SYT-supplied. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.weighted_cs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.weighted_cs","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/KeystoneC8SuperExpBound.lean#L55-L69","doc":"**Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz.** For nonnegative weights `g`,\n`(Σ f g)² ≤ (Σ f² g)(Σ g)`. Proved from `sum_mul_sq_le_sq_mul_sq` applied to\n`f·√g` and `√g`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}